window.SURGERY.sections.push({
  id: "arch",
  category: "aorta",
  title: "Aortic Arch Replacement and Circulatory Arrest (Hemiarch and Total Arch)",
  subtitle: "Rebuilding the segment that feeds the brain — why an open distal anastomosis demands stopping the circulation, and how modern cerebral perfusion makes that survivable.",
  readMins: 28,
  day: 9,
  blocks: [
    {
      title: "Start here — the arch and its branches",
      figures: [{ id: "arch-branch-anatomy", src: "assets/surgery-generated/arch/branch-anatomy.png", width: 1536, height: 1024, alt: "Anterior view of the aortic arch and its three head vessel origins.", caption: "From proximal to distal, the arch gives rise to the brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid, and left subclavian arteries. The brachiocephalic trunk then divides into the right common carotid and right subclavian arteries." }],
      html: "<p>The aortic arch is the short, curved segment that carries blood from the ascending aorta, over the top of the heart, down into the descending thoracic aorta — and along the way it gives off every artery that supplies the brain and the arms. Working from proximal to distal, three vessels normally arise from the arch in this order: the <b>innominate artery</b> (also called the brachiocephalic artery), which itself divides into the right subclavian and right common carotid arteries; the <b>left common carotid artery</b>; and the <b>left subclavian artery</b>. Because these three vessels are the entire blood supply to the brain and arms, any operation that must open, clamp, or replace the arch is simultaneously an operation on the brain's blood supply, and that single fact drives almost everything distinctive about arch surgery.</p><p>Two anatomic variants come up constantly. A <b>bovine arch</b> — present in roughly 15 to 25 percent of people — is not actually a bovine pattern at all but a common human variant in which the innominate and left common carotid arteries share a common origin (or the left common carotid arises directly off the innominate) rather than arising as two separate branches; it matters for surgical planning and for endovascular landing zones but is not itself pathological. An <b>aberrant right subclavian artery (arteria lusoria)</b> arises as the most distal branch off the arch rather than from the innominate, and crosses behind the esophagus to reach the right arm; it can cause dysphagia from esophageal compression (dysphagia lusoria) and is often associated with a <b>Kommerell diverticulum</b>, an outpouching at its origin that itself can dilate, dissect, or rupture.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> Surgeons describe the extent of arch disease and plan branch-vessel reimplantation using the <b>Ishimaru zone</b> system — zone 0 is the ascending aorta proximal to the innominate, zone 1 lies between the innominate and left common carotid, zone 2 between the left common carotid and left subclavian, and the zones continue distally down the descending aorta. Knowing which zone the disease reaches tells you whether a hemiarch will suffice or a total arch with branch reimplantation is required.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Physiology and pathophysiology — why arch disease is a brain problem",
      html: "<p>The brain tolerates remarkably little interruption of its blood supply, and unlike most organs it cannot be protected simply by clamping proximal to it and perfusing everything else, because clamping proximal to the arch vessels would starve the brain entirely. The <b>circle of Willis</b> provides some cross-perfusion between the carotid and vertebrobasilar territories, which is exploited by some cerebral protection strategies, but it is anatomically incomplete or asymmetric in a substantial fraction of people, so it cannot be relied upon uncritically. Arch pathology comes in the same basic forms as elsewhere in the aorta — degenerative aneurysm, acute or chronic dissection extending from the ascending aorta into and through the arch, and diffuse atherosclerotic disease of a heavily calcified, friable \"shaggy\" aorta that is itself a source of embolic stroke — but because the arch cannot simply be cross-clamped and bypassed the way a segment of leg artery can, every one of these processes forces the same fundamental surgical problem: how do you work on a vessel that is also the sole conduit to the brain?</p><div class=\"kbox danger\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Red flag</span> A heavily atherosclerotic, calcified \"shaggy\" arch is a stroke risk in its own right, independent of any aneurysm or dissection — manipulation, clamping, or cannulation near it can dislodge atheromatous debris. Preoperative CT assessment of arch atheroma burden changes cannulation strategy and clamp technique."
    },
    {
      title: "Presentation, workup and indications for surgery",
      html: "<p>Isolated arch aneurysms, like root aneurysms, are usually asymptomatic and found on imaging performed for another reason; when symptomatic, chest, back or interscapular pain suggests expansion, impending rupture, or dissection. Far more often, arch disease is discovered as an extension of disease that starts elsewhere — an ascending aortic aneurysm or a type A dissection that has propagated into and through the arch — and the arch findings then determine how far the repair must extend. <b>CT angiography</b> covering the entire aorta, from root to iliacs, is the essential study: it defines the aneurysm or dissection's proximal and distal extent by Ishimaru zone, the involvement (or sparing) of each head vessel origin, the burden of arch atheroma, and whether disease continues into the descending thoracic aorta in a way that would call for a staged elephant-trunk strategy.</p><p>Indications mirror the root and ascending aorta: a degenerative arch aneurysm is generally repaired around 5.5 cm (lower with connective-tissue disease, rapid growth, or family history of dissection, exactly as for the root), while acute type A dissection extending into the arch is a surgical emergency regardless of diameter because of the risk of rupture, malperfusion and death. Chronic dissection with a progressively enlarging false lumen, or extensive disease spanning from the ascending aorta through the arch into the descending aorta, are the settings where the surgeon must decide up front between a limited hemiarch and a more extensive total arch, possibly combined with a staged descending repair.</p>"
    },
    {
      title: "Why the distal anastomosis must be done open",
      html: "<p>In routine aortic surgery below the arch, the surgeon isolates the segment to be replaced with a cross-clamp, works within a bloodless field distal to the clamp, and the rest of the body continues to be perfused normally by the bypass machine through an unobstructed path. Arch surgery breaks this model. The arch is both the diseased segment and the origin of every vessel supplying the brain, so a clamp placed across it (or across its branches) to create a bloodless field would simultaneously stop all flow to the brain — and a clamp placed on a friable, aneurysmal or dissected arch wall risks tearing or fracturing calcified plaque into the cerebral circulation. There is, in short, no place to put a clamp that both excludes the diseased segment and preserves cerebral perfusion.</p><p>The solution is to perform the <b>distal anastomosis open</b> — with no clamp on the arch at all — which means the surgeon needs a completely still, bloodless, unpressurized field to sew into, and the only way to achieve that without a clamp is to temporarily stop the circulation to the whole body. This is <b>circulatory arrest</b>: pump flow is stopped entirely for a defined period while the arch is opened, the head vessels are inspected and managed, and the distal graft anastomosis is completed under direct vision. Because whole-body circulatory arrest also means the brain receives no flow (unless a dedicated cerebral perfusion strategy is layered on top), everything about modern arch surgery is organized around making that arrested interval survivable for the brain.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> The core logic of arch surgery in one sentence: you cannot safely clamp a diseased arch without threatening the brain, so the distal anastomosis is done open under circulatory arrest, and cerebral protection strategies exist specifically to buy time for that open, clampless window.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Deep hypothermic circulatory arrest and the safe-time relationship",
      html: "<p>Stopping all blood flow to the brain is tolerated only because cooling dramatically slows cellular metabolism, and the classical technique — <b>deep hypothermic circulatory arrest (DHCA)</b> — cools the whole patient via the cardiopulmonary bypass heat exchanger to a core temperature of roughly 18 to 20 degrees Celsius before flow is stopped. Cooling reduces the cerebral metabolic rate of oxygen consumption (CMRO2) steeply with temperature, so a deeply cooled brain can survive without any blood flow for a meaningfully longer period than a normothermic one. At 18 degrees, the classically quoted \"safe\" arrest time before the risk of neurologic injury rises appreciably is on the order of <b>30 to 40 minutes</b>; beyond that window, the incidence of stroke and, even more so, of transient neurologic dysfunction climbs progressively.</p><p>Deep cooling is not free, however — reaching and rewarming from 18 to 20 degrees takes time on bypass, prolongs the whole operation, and produces its own coagulopathy (cold-inactivated clotting enzymes and platelets), organ dysfunction, and inflammatory activation. This has driven the modern shift toward <b>moderate hypothermia</b> (roughly 24 to 28 degrees) combined with active <b>cerebral perfusion</b> during the arrest interval: because the brain is being separately perfused rather than relying solely on cold-induced metabolic suppression, the body does not need to be cooled as deeply, shortening bypass time while still protecting the brain — and often the rest of the body as well, since moderate hypothermia still affords some systemic protection during the brief periods when even the cerebral perfusion is interrupted.</p><div class=\"kbox danger\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Red flag</span> \"Safe\" DHCA time is a guideline, not a guarantee — age, pre-existing cerebrovascular disease, and incomplete circles of Willis all shorten the individual patient's true tolerance. Exceeding the target arrest window is a recognized, dose-dependent risk factor for both stroke and temporary neurologic dysfunction.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Cerebral protection strategies — antegrade and retrograde perfusion, and NIRS",
      figures: [{ id: "arch-cerebral-perfusion", src: "assets/surgery-generated/arch/cerebral-perfusion-v2.png", width: 1774, height: 887, alt: "Paired aortic arch artery schematics with right axillary cannulation alone and with an additional left common carotid cannula.", caption: "Right axillary inflow can perfuse the right carotid through the brachiocephalic artery during open arch repair. A separate left carotid cannula adds direct left-sided antegrade perfusion when bilateral perfusion is chosen." }],
      html: "<p><b>Antegrade cerebral perfusion (ACP)</b> is the dominant modern strategy: during circulatory arrest, a separate cannula — most often placed in the right axillary or innominate artery, sometimes with direct cannulation of a head vessel — continues to deliver oxygenated, cooled blood forward, in the normal direction, into the cerebral circulation while the rest of the body is arrested. <b>Unilateral ACP</b> perfuses only the right side (via the axillary/innominate route) and relies on a patent circle of Willis to distribute flow to the left hemisphere as well; it is simpler and adequate for most arch cases of moderate duration. <b>Bilateral ACP</b> adds a second cannula, typically to the left common carotid artery, to perfuse both hemispheres independently — used for longer anticipated arch times, an incomplete or asymmetric circle of Willis, or when unilateral perfusion proves inadequate by monitoring.</p><p><b>Retrograde cerebral perfusion (RCP)</b> takes the opposite approach: cold oxygenated blood is infused backward through a cannula in the superior vena cava, flowing retrograde through the cerebral venous system. It provides some hypothermic protection and may help wash embolic debris out of the cerebral circulation, but it does not deliver nutritive, physiologic antegrade flow to brain tissue the way ACP does, and it has been largely supplanted by ACP as the primary strategy for anything beyond short arrest times — RCP is now used mainly as an adjunct or a fallback when antegrade cannulation is not feasible.</p><p><b>Near-infrared spectroscopy (NIRS)</b> cerebral oximetry is the standard real-time monitor during arch surgery: sensors on the forehead estimate regional cerebral oxygen saturation non-invasively. A significant fall — commonly flagged at roughly a 20 percent relative drop from the patient's own baseline, or an absolute value falling into the low-to-mid 40s to 50s percent range — is a warning sign of inadequate or asymmetric cerebral perfusion (malpositioned cannula, inadequate flow, or an incomplete circle of Willis failing to cross-perfuse the unmonitored side) and prompts immediate corrective action, such as repositioning the cannula or converting to bilateral perfusion.</p><div class=\"kbox mnemonic\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Mnemonic</span> Antegrade = forward, physiologic, the current standard; think \"axillary in, brain fed.\" Retrograde = backward through the SVC, mainly a cooling/debris-washout adjunct now, not a primary nutritive strategy. NIRS is the real-time gauge that tells you whether whichever strategy you chose is actually working.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "The operation — hemiarch versus total arch",
      html: "<p>Once cooled to target temperature and in circulatory arrest with cerebral perfusion running, the surgeon opens the arch and directly inspects the wall and the head-vessel origins — this direct inspection is itself one of the advantages of the open technique, since disease extent can be confirmed rather than inferred from imaging alone. A <b>hemiarch</b> replacement addresses only the undersurface (lesser curvature) of the arch immediately adjacent to the ascending aorta, without touching the origins of any of the three head vessels; the open distal anastomosis is fashioned just proximal to the innominate artery. This is the appropriate, more limited operation whenever the aneurysm or dissection does not extend into the head-vessel origins themselves — the majority of ascending aortic aneurysms and most acute type A dissections that require any arch extension at all.</p><p>A <b>total arch</b> replacement is required when disease involves the origins of the head vessels themselves, or when the entire arch is aneurysmal. Here all three vessels must be divided and reimplanted into the new graft, using one of two techniques. The <b>island technique</b> leaves the three ostia attached to a single common patch (\"island\") of native aortic wall, which is then sewn as one unit into a matching opening in the graft — technically simpler and faster, but it leaves a broad island of potentially diseased native tissue in the repair and reperfuses all three vessels simultaneously. The <b>branched-graft technique</b> uses a graft with individual side-branch limbs, each vessel divided and sewn separately to its own branch — technically more demanding and slower, but it removes all native arch tissue around the ostia, allows differential (sequential) reperfusion of each vessel as its anastomosis is completed (shortening that vessel's own individual ischemic time), and is generally preferred when the head-vessel origins themselves are diseased or aneurysmal.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> Hemiarch = disease confined to the undersurface near the ascending aorta, head vessels untouched, distal anastomosis just proximal to the innominate. Total arch = disease reaches the head-vessel origins, all three vessels reimplanted as an island or via a branched graft. The extent of disease on preoperative CT, by Ishimaru zone, is what decides between them before the patient ever reaches the operating room.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Extending the repair — elephant trunk and frozen elephant trunk",
      figures: [{ id: "arch-elephant-trunk", src: "assets/surgery-generated/arch/elephant-trunk.png", width: 1536, height: 1024, alt: "Paired arch grafts showing a free fabric sleeve and an expanded stented extension in the descending aorta.", caption: "Left: a conventional elephant trunk leaves a free fabric sleeve within the descending aorta for later repair. Right: a frozen elephant trunk adds a stented segment that expands against the descending aortic wall during the arch operation." }],
      html: "<p>When disease extends well beyond the arch into the descending thoracic aorta, replacing everything from the root to the mid-descending aorta in a single operation under one long period of circulatory arrest is often more than a patient can safely tolerate. The classic <b>(open) elephant trunk</b> technique solves this by staging the repair: at the first operation, the ascending aorta and arch are replaced in the usual way, but instead of completing the distal anastomosis to normal descending aorta, a segment of graft is left <b>free-floating, unattached, hanging down into the descending aorta</b> like a trunk. Weeks to months later, once the patient has recovered, a second-stage procedure — increasingly performed endovascularly rather than through a second open thoracotomy — connects a new graft or stent-graft to this free trunk, completing the descending repair with a well-defined, pre-positioned docking site rather than having to find and control a diseased native aorta from scratch.</p><p>The <b>frozen elephant trunk (FET)</b> is the modern hybrid evolution: instead of leaving a floppy free graft segment for a separate later operation, a self-expanding covered stent-graft is deployed antegrade into the proximal descending aorta at the time of the arch operation itself, sewn into the same distal anastomosis that completes the arch graft. This combines a durable, hand-sewn proximal anastomosis with an immediately deployed stented \"landing zone\" that either treats descending disease outright in a single stage or at minimum creates a clean docking zone for a straightforward future TEVAR (thoracic endovascular aortic repair) extension, often avoiding the need for a formal second open operation altogether.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> Elephant trunk (open, free-floating graft) and frozen elephant trunk (stented, deployed at the time of the index operation) both exist to solve the same problem — extensive disease that would make a single ascending-through-descending repair too long and too morbid — but FET converts what used to require two open operations into, usually, one open operation plus a much smaller endovascular completion.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Complications and how to manage them",
      html: "<p><b>Stroke</b> is the complication that defines this operation's risk profile, and it has two distinct mechanisms that call for different prevention strategies. <b>Embolic stroke</b> results from atheromatous debris or air dislodged during cannulation, clamping, or manipulation of a diseased arch — minimized by careful preoperative assessment of arch atheroma on CT, thoughtful cannulation site selection, and meticulous de-airing. <b>Malperfusion-related (watershed) stroke</b> results from genuinely inadequate cerebral blood flow during the arrest and perfusion interval — from an arrest time that exceeded safe limits, a malpositioned or kinked perfusion cannula, or an incomplete circle of Willis failing to cross-perfuse during unilateral ACP — minimized by NIRS monitoring, appropriate choice between unilateral and bilateral ACP, and disciplined arrest-time targets. <b>Temporary neurologic dysfunction (TND)</b> — postoperative confusion, agitation, or delirium without a persistent focal deficit, typically resolving over days — is distinct from stroke, more common with longer arrest times, and thought to reflect a milder, reversible degree of the same ischemic stress rather than a true infarct.</p><p><b>Spinal cord ischemia and paraplegia</b> become a real risk whenever the repair extends well into the descending thoracic aorta, because doing so sacrifices segmental intercostal arteries that can supply the anterior spinal artery, including the dominant <b>artery of Adamkiewicz</b> in some patients. Risk is reduced by cerebrospinal fluid drainage (lowering CSF pressure to preserve spinal cord perfusion pressure), maintaining an adequate mean arterial pressure post-operatively, minimizing the length of aorta covered, and staging extensive repairs (exactly the rationale behind the elephant trunk strategies) rather than covering the entire thoracic aorta in one sitting. The <b>left recurrent laryngeal nerve</b>, which loops posteriorly around the aortic arch near the ligamentum arteriosum, and the <b>phrenic nerve</b>, which runs along the pericardium in the operative field, are both at risk of direct injury during arch mobilization — RLN injury presents as hoarseness and vocal cord paresis, phrenic injury as hemidiaphragm paralysis. <b>Bleeding and coagulopathy</b> after prolonged bypass and circulatory arrest reflect both dilutional and hypothermia-induced platelet and clotting-factor dysfunction, compounded by the multiple graft and reimplantation suture lines of a total arch; management is rewarming, factor and platelet correction, and re-exploration for a persistent surgical source.</p><div class=\"kbox danger\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Red flag</span> New hoarseness after an arch operation is not automatically attributed to the endotracheal tube — the left recurrent laryngeal nerve's course around the arch near the ligamentum arteriosum puts it directly at risk during arch mobilization, and persistent hoarseness warrants laryngoscopic evaluation.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Pimping ceiling — zones, numbers and named strategies",
      html: "<p>Know the branch order of the arch cold — innominate, then left common carotid, then left subclavian — and the two classic variants: a bovine arch (shared innominate/left carotid origin, present in roughly 15 to 25 percent of people) and an aberrant right subclavian artery with its associated Kommerell diverticulum. Know the <b>Ishimaru zones</b> (0 through the ascending aorta/arch, extending distally through the descending aorta) as the vocabulary for describing disease extent and planning hemiarch versus total arch versus staged descending repair. Know why a clampless, open distal anastomosis is required at all — a diseased arch cannot be safely clamped without either fracturing atheroma or occluding cerebral flow — and that circulatory arrest exists specifically to create a bloodless field for that anastomosis.</p><p>Know the numbers: deep hypothermic circulatory arrest to roughly 18 to 20 degrees Celsius, with a classic safe window of about 30 to 40 minutes before neurologic injury risk climbs; the modern trend toward moderate hypothermia (24 to 28 degrees) paired with active antegrade cerebral perfusion to shorten total bypass time while still protecting the brain. Know unilateral versus bilateral ACP (bilateral for longer anticipated arch times or an incomplete circle of Willis) and that retrograde cerebral perfusion via the SVC is now mainly an adjunct rather than a primary strategy. Know that NIRS flags a roughly 20 percent relative drop from baseline as actionable. Know hemiarch (head vessels untouched) versus total arch (island or branched-graft reimplantation of all three vessels) and elephant trunk (staged, free-floating graft) versus frozen elephant trunk (single-stage, stented at the index operation) as the answer to disease extending into the descending aorta. And know the complication list cold: embolic versus malperfusion stroke, TND, spinal cord ischemia and the artery of Adamkiewicz, and left recurrent laryngeal and phrenic nerve injury from arch mobilization.</p><div class=\"kbox mnemonic\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Mnemonic</span> The arch operation in one line: <b>cool (moderate or deep), arrest (stop the body), perfuse the brain (ACP unilateral/bilateral, or RCP as adjunct), open and sew (hemiarch or total arch, island or branched), extend if needed (elephant trunk or frozen elephant trunk), then reperfuse and rewarm.</b></div>"
    }
  ],
  steps: [
    "Position supine, perform median sternotomy, and expose the right axillary or innominate artery selected from preoperative imaging",
    "Heparinize, cannulate the chosen axillary or innominate artery for inflow, cannulate the right atrium for drainage, and place an LV vent",
    "Initiate cardiopulmonary bypass and cool to moderate hypothermia while monitoring NIRS and preparing the head vessels",
    "Cross-clamp the ascending aorta, deliver cardioplegia, and complete the planned proximal root or ascending repair during cooling",
    "At target temperature, snare the head vessels and start antegrade cerebral perfusion through the axillary or innominate inflow",
    "Stop systemic flow to begin lower-body circulatory arrest while maintaining monitored antegrade cerebral perfusion",
    "Remove the cross-clamp, open the arch, and excise the diseased tissue for the planned total arch replacement",
    "Transect the distal arch at the chosen healthy landing zone and prepare the descending aorta",
    "Sew the distal end of a branched arch graft to the descending aorta as an open anastomosis",
    "De-air the graft, clamp it proximal to its perfusion side arm, and restore lower-body flow through the side arm",
    "Begin controlled rewarming once lower-body perfusion is restored",
    "Reimplant the left subclavian artery into its graft branch while maintaining cerebral perfusion",
    "Reimplant the left common carotid artery and confirm left-sided cerebral oxygenation",
    "Reimplant the innominate artery last, then confirm symmetric cerebral perfusion and head-vessel flow",
    "Complete the proximal graft anastomosis to the repaired ascending aorta or root while myocardial protection continues",
    "De-air the heart and graft, remove the graft clamp, restore coronary and full systemic flow, and then discontinue cerebral perfusion",
    "Wean from bypass, exclude malperfusion with TEE and monitoring, reverse heparin, secure hemostasis, place drains and wires, and close"
  ]
});

